### 6.3 MTD related efficiencies

The MTD related efficiencies include the MTD trigger efficiency and MTD response efficiency. Three sources are considered for estimating the systematic uncertainty of the MTD trigger efficiency:

1. Use double-sided Crystal ball function to fit the \(\Delta TACSum\) distribution in the Run17.
2. Shift the Run17 the \(\Delta TACSum\) cut then applied on the Run15 \(\Delta V_{z}\) distribution to estimate the MTD online time window cut efficiency.
3. Smear the Run17 the \(\Delta TACSum\) cut then applied on the Run15 \(\Delta V_{z}\) distribution to estimate the MTD online time window cut efficiency.

The efficiencies from these three sources are shown in Fig. 6.4. The systematic uncertainty for the MTD trigger efficiency is calculated by comparing with each corrected number of \(J/\psi\) with the corresponding MTD trigger efficiencies correction and it is about 1.5% for all the \(J/\psi\)\(p_{T}\) bins.

The source for MTD response efficiency is from the difference between each backleg efficiency, and the efficiency template in individual MTD module is used to estimate the systematic uncertainty for the MTD response efficiency. The systematics uncertainty from the MTD response efficiency is shown in Fig 6.6 and it is less than 1%, which can be negligible.

